From 34ff953ff2a4d786820c6a1e16ebc202b61b42bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: T0 <dev@local>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 15:44:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] T6: controllers, routing, and fix F3 (branch-delete guard)

Presentation layer for API 4.10, plus the last flagged pre-work item.

Controllers (all [Authorize] only, no permission attributes, per the
locked authorization decision):

- TransactionsController [api/inventory/transactions]: list, detail,
  create, receive, dispose. Every write rejects a system-admin caller
  (decision Q7); reads are open to admin across tenants.
- InventoryHistoryController [api/inventory/history]: thin alias over the
  same ITransferService.GetAllAsync call as the transactions list - kept
  as its own controller rather than a second route on TransactionsController
  so routing tables and Swagger don't have one implying it owns the other.
- CardDisposalsController [api/inventory]: cards/dispose (create),
  disposals + disposals/{id} (list/detail). Full paths given per action
  since the two resources don't share a parent segment. Never available
  to a system admin - no read-only path here at all, unlike transfers.

All three are one-liners per action, matching every existing controller's
own convention: (await _services.X.YAsync(...)).ToActionResult(this).
No new response-mapping code, no manual ApiResponse construction.

Fix F3: BranchService.SoftDeleteAsync now enforces the rule API 4.5
already documented but never checked - a branch holding non-zero stock,
or party to an in-progress transfer, cannot be deleted. Backed by two
new repo methods: IStockRepo.HasNonZeroStockAsync and the
ICardTransferRepo.HasInProgressTransferAsync pre-built back in T3
specifically for this. Both checks run before the actor lookup - no
point resolving who's deleting a branch that can't be deleted.

Localization: 2 new keys (Branch.HasStock, Branch.HasInProgressTransfer)
in both languages. Note for the record, not something this patch touches:
the pre-existing Branch.* error codes (NotFound, AlreadyDeleted, etc.)
have no resx entries at all and silently fall back to their English
default per ApiError.Message's own doc comment - a gap that predates
this work and is outside F3's scope.

No migration - this phase is pure application/infrastructure/presentation
logic on top of what T1's schema already provides.
